  "solution": {
    "text": "<p>Given:</p><p> <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mfrac><mrow><mi>a</mi><msup><mi>x</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>+</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>(</mo><mi>a</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>)</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>9</mn><mi>a</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow><mrow><msup><mi>x</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>-</mo><mn>8</mn><mi>x</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>32</mn></mrow></mfrac><mo>&lt;</mo><mn>0</mn><mo> </mo><mo> </mo><mo>∀</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>R</mi></math></p><p>For quadratic <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msup><mi>x</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>-</mo><mn>8</mn><mi>x</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>32</mn><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mo> </mo><mo> </mo><msub><mi>D</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><msup><mfenced><mrow><mo>-</mo><mn>8</mn></mrow></mfenced><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>-</mo><mn>4</mn><mfenced><mn>32</mn></mfenced><mo>=</mo><mo>-</mo><mn>64</mn></math></p><p>Since the discriminant is less than zero and the leading coefficient is positive, this quadratic will always be positive.</p><p>Now, solving <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>a</mi><msup><mi>x</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>+</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>(</mo><mi>a</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>)</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>9</mn><mi>a</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>4</mn><mo>&lt;</mo><mn>0</mn></math></p><p>We know that, for a quadratic to be always negative, the coefficient of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msup><mi>x</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>&lt;</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mo> </mo><mi>D</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><mn>0</mn></math>.</p><p><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mo>⇒</mo><mi>a</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><mn>0</mn></math> </p><p>But we want positive values.</p><p>So, no positive integral value exist.</p>",
